ment did  open for valentine's day but says it  wasn't worth it financially or  emotionally we were still dealing with  the excess incessant noise the fireworks  the honking the drinking people  urinating and defecating on the patio  a lot of businesses in ottawa's byward  market stayed open during the protest  but lost money public transportation was  shut down meaning people couldn't make  it and a lot of the ones who could were  nervous  erica gary didn't close but he lost  about 80 percent of his customers people  were scared to come down  so we have minimum we basically we're  open  not to stay at home and go crazy  now life is still far from back to  normal the security zone around  parliament hill is smaller but it's  still a chunk of downtown that's  surrounded by an eight-foot fence and  police checkpoints it's just been insane  these men live in the security zone and  ventured out for the first time tuesday  still a little in shock it was it was  really brutal there was no police there  was no  neighbors were caring for each other  but we were forgotten  as if this area hadn't been through  enough there was a police operation on  this day the mall that's been closed for  three weeks was closed again for a  suspected armed shoplifter people who  had been out of work were out on the  streets  a lot of people in this city were  shaking their heads wondering what's  next the return to normalcy  isn't a straight line  mike armstrong global news ottawa  and that is global national for this  tuesday february 22nd a  once-in-a-lifetime date it is  22-22 a palindrome day because it reads  the same four words as backwards it's  also an ambergram because on a  calculator it can be read upside down in  exactly the same way  so it's both a tuesday and a tuesday  to some the lineup of twos is just  mind-blowing people are matching it with  temperatures with birthdays with  speedometers  and with time the ultimate is when the  clock hits 22-22 tonight for some  businesse